
What is Iterable?
--> Iterable is the root interface of the Collection framework
    that provides the iterator() method to enable traversal using the for-each loop.
    It is present in java.lang package.

public interface Iterable<T> {
    Iterator<T> iterator();
}

Q. When was Iterable introduced?
--> Introduced in Java 1.5
    Along with Generics and for-each loop
📌 It has only one abstract method → iterator(), return type also iterator.

Iterable<Integer> ref = new ArrayList<>();     // only iterable methode accsess (parent child relation)
  ref.add(10); ❌                                  parent ref not give child methods otherwise CTE
  ref.remove(10); ❌

Iterator<Integer> it = ref.iterator(); ✅

The main purpose of Iterable is to allow traversal of a collection using the for-each loop.
Without Iterable:
for(Object o : collection) ❌ compile-time error

With Iterable:
for(Integer i : list){  ✅
System.out.println(i);
}

Enhanced for loop(for each loop) : Internally worked

Iterator<Integer> it = list.iterator();
while(it.hasNext()) {
    Integer i = it.next();
    System.out.println(i);
}

Iterable provides - iterator() method which is abstract method and
                    iterator interface which have hasNext() and next() methods.

Iterable
   ↑
Collection
   ↑
List, Set, Queue

Q. Which classes implement Iterable?
--> All collection classes implement Iterable indirectly:

✔️ ArrayList
✔️ LinkedList
✔️ HashSet
✔️ TreeSet
✔️ Vector
✔️ Stack

❌ Map does NOT implement Iterable directly

